<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
Constructing a system command with unvalidated user input may allow an attacker
to choose an executable or change the meaning of its arguments. If a command
string is passed to a shell, malicious input may also execute additional commands.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
If possible, avoid invoking a shell. Use a fixed executable and pass arguments
separately using <code>Process</code>. Validate untrusted values against the
allowed values for the command and its arguments.
</p>
<p>
If a shell command is required, select from an allowlist of complete, fixed
commands rather than concatenating untrusted input. Do not rely solely on
removing shell metacharacters.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
The following example passes user input to <code>bash -c</code> without
validating it first:
</p>
<sample src="CommandInjectionBad.swift" />
<p>
The following example allows only a fixed set of command strings before
passing one to the shell:
</p>
<sample src="CommandInjectionGood.swift" />
</example>

<references>
<li>
OWASP:
<a href="https://community.owasp.org/attacks/Command_Injection">Command Injection</a>.
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
